  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-tgr-framework-units": [
      {
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Explicit primitive/derived quantities, mechanical versus energy covectors, proper versus KillingT, scalar/vectorβ and KMS time-length width, geometric/accelerationκ.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      "## Definition\n\nUse a smooth, oriented and time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold $(M,g)$ of signature $(-,+,+,+)$ with its metric-selected Levi-Civita connection $\\nabla$, as defined in [[def-gr-smooth-lorentzian-objects]] and [[thm-gr-nondegenerate-levi-civita-and-tensor-derivative]]. A chart is a smooth coordinate map; an observer congruence is a smooth future-directed unit timelike vector field $u$ with $g(u,u)=-1$; its integral curves, an adapted chart, and a tetrad field are distinct objects. The exact regularity and rest-space conventions of [[def-tgr-local-fluid-state]] and the chart/congruence distinction of [[thm-sr-local-congruence-adapted-charts-and-frobenius]] apply. An orthogonal family of rest slices is not available for every congruence.\n\nFor physical unit assignments use length coordinates $x^0=ct_{\\rm phys}$ and spatial coordinates in metres, where $c>0$ has units $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$. In this convention the metric components, a unit observer $u$, and a length-normalized Killing field $\\xi$ are dimensionless. Other coordinate choices transform component units by their coordinate Jacobians. State maps and geometric currents have the types and units in [[def-tgr-local-fluid-state]]: a formal marker density $n$ is $\\mathrm{marker\\,m^{-3}}$; entropy density $s$ is $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,m^{-3}}$; a $C^1$ entropy current $S^a$ has components $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,m^{-3}}$; and its physical flux is $cS^a$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,m^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}$. Its geometric divergence $\\nabla_aS^a$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}\\,m^{-4}}$, so $c\\nabla_aS^a$ is entropy production per physical volume per second. The symmetric stress tensor $\\mathsf T^{ab}$ has components in pascals in length coordinates; it is a tensor, not an observer-independent array of energy densities. The state-function energy density $\\varepsilon$ and the observer projection $\\mathsf T^{ab}u_a u_b$ remain distinct unless a material model identifies them.\n\nThe constant $c$ supplies the spacetime length/time conversion. In a branch with dynamical Einstein gravity, $G$ is an independent coupling parameter in $\\mathrm{m^3\\,kg^{-1}\\,s^{-2}}$. The Boltzmann constant $k_B$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$ and is used when a statistical entropy/temperature calibration is adopted. Planck's constant $h_{\\rm P}=2\\pi\\hbar$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$; $\\hbar$ is used only in a quantum branch. Under the current SI definition, $c$, $h_{\\rm P}$ and $k_B$ have exact defining numerical values; this metrological fact does not make them derived predictions of a thermodynamic or gravitational model. $G$ is experimentally adjusted and carries measurement uncertainty. The roles of these quantities as primitive model inputs are framework-specific. The local energy function, pressure, temperature and chemical potential in [[def-tgr-local-fluid-state]] are derived from the supplied state function; no particle mass or universal equation of state is derived from gravity alone.\n\nFor a smooth Killing field $\\xi$ on an open domain, meaning $\\mathcal L_\\xi g=0$ by [[def-gr-killing-field]], restrict attention to a domain where $\\xi$ is future timelike and choose a positive normalization at infinity or at a specified wall/observer. Define the lapse $N_\\xi:=\\sqrt{-g(\\xi,\\xi)}>0$ and unit observer $u_\\xi:=\\xi/N_\\xi$. For a supplied proper-temperature scalar $T>0$ in kelvins, define the Killing-normalized temperature field $T_\\xi:=N_\\xi T$. It is not assumed constant; if a separate equilibrium argument proves constancy on its stated domain, denote that value by $T_K$. The finite matter charge and its side-flux convention are those in [[lem-tgr-killing-energy-normalization]] and [[def-tgr-spacelike-current-slab]]; their charge-balance clause retains its explicitly conditional finite-slab hypotheses. This charge is not a general gravitational energy.\n\nKeep covector types separate. A mechanical momentum covector $p_a$ has units $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$; its Killing energy is $E_\\xi(p):=-c\\,p_a\\xi^a$ in joules. An energy-valued covector $\\pi_a$ already has units joules and pairs as $E_\\xi(\\pi):=-\\pi_a\\xi^a$, without another factor of $c$. An inverse-length wave covector $k_a$ has units $\\mathrm{m^{-1}}$ and pairs as $E_\\xi(k):=-\\hbar c\\,k_a\\xi^a$. For a specified charged stationary gauge, charge $q$ has coulombs and a potential covector $A_a$ in length coordinates has units $\\mathrm{V\\,s\\,m^{-1}}$; when a global stationary potential is actually defined, $\\Phi_\\xi:=-cA_a\\xi^a$ has volts and $q\\Phi_\\xi$ has joules. A charged energy or chemical-potential convention requires its stated gauge and reference; no global potential, electrochemical equilibrium, or constancy is asserted here.\n\nWhen $T_K>0$ is supplied, distinguish the inverse-temperature vector $\\beta^a:=u_\\xi^a/(k_BT)=\\xi^a/(k_BT_K)$, with units $\\mathrm{J^{-1}}$, from the scalar $\\beta_K:=1/(k_BT_K)$, also in $\\mathrm{J^{-1}}$. For a specified algebra of bounded observables, a strongly continuous one-parameter automorphism group $\\alpha_t$, and a positive normalized state $\\omega$, the KMS condition at parameter width $b>0$ means that for every pair $A,B$ in the declared analytic subalgebra there is a function $F_{A,B}$ continuous on $0\\leq\\operatorname{Im}z\\leq b$, analytic on the open strip, and satisfying $F_{A,B}(t)=\\omega(A\\alpha_t(B))$ and $F_{A,B}(t+ib)=\\omega(\\alpha_t(B)A)$. For physical-time flow $\\alpha_t(B)=e^{itH_K/\\hbar}Be^{-itH_K/\\hbar}$ the KMS width is $\\hbar\\beta_K$ seconds. If the flow parameter is length $\\lambda=ct$, then $\\alpha_\\lambda(B)=e^{i\\lambda H_K/(\\hbar c)}Be^{-i\\lambda H_K/(\\hbar c)}$ and the width is $\\hbar c\\beta_K$ metres. These are unit and convention assignments only; no KMS state or equilibrium flow is asserted to exist.\n\nOn a specified smooth null hypersurface $\\mathcal H$, a nonzero null tangent generator $\\chi$ may admit a scalar $\\kappa_{\\rm geom}$ defined by $\\nabla_\\chi\\chi=\\kappa_{\\rm geom}\\chi$. When the flow parameter has length units, $\\kappa_{\\rm geom}$ has units $\\mathrm{m^{-1}}$; the corresponding acceleration-unit quantity $\\kappa_{\\rm acc}:=c^2\\kappa_{\\rm geom}$ has units $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-2}}$. This definition does not assert a horizon exists, that $\\kappa$ is constant, or that a temperature law follows. The formula $T_K=\\hbar c\\kappa_{\\rm geom}/(2\\pi k_B)$ requires a specified quantum/semiclassical model, and an area-entropy formula requires a separate thermodynamic identification. In particular, equal units do not identify local material entropy, finite statistical entropy, and area entropy, and no generalized second law is asserted here."
    ],
    "post-tgr-local-material-thermodynamics": [
      {
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Fixed-composition C2e(v,σ),positiveT,restenergy and operational meanings; no global-gravity additivity, microscopic derivation or universal EOS.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      "## Postulate\n\nFor a selected fixed-composition, single-material branch, adopt a local-equilibrium description on an open state domain $D\\subset(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R$ with a supplied function $e\\in C^2(D,\\mathbb R)$ and $T:=\\partial_{\\sigma_p}e>0$. Here $v$ is the rest volume per selected material marker, $\\sigma_p$ is entropy per marker, and $e$ is energy per marker, including the rest-energy contribution; their types and SI units are those in [[def-tgr-local-fluid-state]] and [[def-tgr-framework-units]]. For a small comoving parcel with marker count $N_p$, proper volume $V$, and entropy $S$, this model assigns energy $E=N_p e(V/N_p,S/N_p)$. The physical interpretation of the marker as a particle count is part of this fixed-composition material model, not a consequence of the mathematical marker definition.\n\nThis is an adopted constitutive/local-equilibrium premise for the stated material and regime. It does not derive $e$ microscopically, assert a measured equation of state, impose a spacetime conservation equation, imply a universal gas law, or make the local parcel energy additive with the energy of an arbitrary self-gravitating region. Rest energy belongs in $e$; if a rest mass $m$ is specified, its contribution is $mc^2$, not $m$ in SI units. No entropy-maximization, quantum Gibbs representation, or global gravitating thermodynamic limit is assumed."
    ],
    "post-tgr-local-entropy-admissibility": [
      {
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Given defined smooth current and material model,∇S≥0; not a geometry theorem, globalGSL or statistical/horizon identification.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      "## Postulate\n\nOn an open region of the smooth spacetime of [[def-tgr-framework-units]], take a separately specified material/current model in its declared smooth regime. Its entropy current is a supplied $C^1$ vector field $S^a$ of the type and units in [[def-tgr-local-fluid-state]]. Adopt the local admissibility condition\n\n$$\\nabla_aS^a\\geq 0$$\n\nthroughout that region. This inequality is a physical restriction on the chosen model and current; it is not a construction of an undefined constitutive current. Each application must state its current, regime, boundary/exchange data, and any source or sink terms it includes.\n\nThe inequality is not deduced from Lorentzian geometry, the local state function, or finite data. It does not assert statistical thermalization, a universal microscopic $H$-theorem, entropy-area identification, or a generalized second law. Romatschke's cited calculation is restricted to a specified conformal-fluid model and itself records the absence of experimental verification known to that author; that source limitation does not turn this postulate into an observed law. No experimental observation or uncertainty is supplied by this item."
    ],
    "pthm-tgr-finite-entropy-balance": [
      {
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Supplied smooth slab/current and local secondlaw; proper totalentropy plus export equals nonnegative production; sideimpermeability needed for monotonicity.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      "## Statement\n\nUse the smooth time-oriented Lorentzian geometry, positively oriented chart and product slab $K=[x^0_-,x^0_+]\\times\\Omega$ with open spatial rectangle $\\Omega$ and compact closure $\\overline K$ of [[def-tgr-spacelike-current-slab]], with nonempty rectangular factors, spacelike slices, future unit normal $n$, induced volume $dV_h$, and a $C^1$ current defined on a neighborhood of $\\overline K$. Let $S^a$ be the entropy current selected by [[post-tgr-local-entropy-admissibility]] and put $\\Pi:=\\nabla_aS^a\\geq0$ on $K$. Define the proper slice entropy\n\n$$Q_S(x^0):=\\int_\\Omega[-g(S,n)]\\,dV_h$$\n\nand let $\\Phi_S(B)$ be the net outward flux through the union $B$ of the six spatial side faces, with the sign convention of [[def-tgr-spacelike-current-slab]]. Conditional on the exact current statement and hypotheses of the **draft** mathematical theorem [[thm-tgr-finite-current-production-balance]],\n\n$$Q_S(x^0_+)-Q_S(x^0_-)+\\Phi_S(B)=\\int_K\\Pi\\,\\mathrm{vol}_g\\geq0.$$\n\nEvery term has units $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$ in the length-coordinate convention of [[def-tgr-framework-units]]. If the net outward side flux is zero, the proper slice entropy is nondecreasing. This is a deduction conditional on both the adopted local inequality and the actual draft supplier interface; it is not an observation, a proof approval of that mathematical supplier, or a generalized second law."
    ],
    "texp-tgr-insulated-entropy-region": [
      {
        "kind": "thought-experiment",
        "domain": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Conditional ideal boundary; growth equals integrated production and zero production gives equal endpoint charge; no observations/GSL.",
